[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"DIP: Abstractions vs Implementations and Inversion of Control (IoC)"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the Dependency Inversion Principle"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) is the 'D' in the SOLID principles of object-oriented design. It states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. Furthermore, abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions."},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High-level modules: Business logic and core functionality."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Low-level modules: Database operations, network calls, and file I/O."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Abstractions: Interfaces or abstract classes that define contracts."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem: Tight Coupling"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a standard application where a high-level reporting class directly instantiates a low-level MySQL database class. This creates tight coupling, making the system difficult to test and inflexible to change."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class MySqlDatabase {
  public void save(String data) {
    // Saving to MySQL
  }
}

class ReportManager {
  private MySqlDatabase database = new MySqlDatabase();

  public void generateReport(String data) {
    database.save(data);
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"The Solution: Introducing Abstractions"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To decouple the classes, we introduce an abstraction (an interface) that both the high-level and low-level modules will depend on."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"interface Database {
  void save(String data);
}

class MySqlDatabase implements Database {
  public void save(String data) {
    // Saving to MySQL
  }
}

class MongoDatabase implements Database {
  public void save(String data) {
    // Saving to MongoDB
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Inversion of Control (IoC)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inversion of Control is a design pattern where the control of object creation and dependency resolution is shifted from the application code to an external framework or container. By using dependency injection, we pass the implementation into the high-level class rather than having the class create it."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"class ReportManager {
  private final Database database;

  // Dependency Injection via constructor
  public ReportManager(Database database) {
    this.database = database;
  }

  public void generateReport(String data) {
    database.save(data);
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Benefits"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: Easily swap implementations (e.g., swapping MySqlDatabase for MongoDatabase without modifying ReportManager)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Testability: Pass mock implementations of the Database interface during unit testing."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintainability: Changes to low-level details do not ripple up to high-level business logic."}]
